            <div xml:id="d244">

                <pb facs="0249" n="242" sameAs="frus:frus1937v03/pg242"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/8919: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Counselor of Embassy in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Peck</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-07-22T14:00:00+08:00">July 22, 1937—2
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received July 22—5:46 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>317. An American-educated Chinese Army officer who is acting as liaison between
                    the Chinese General Staff and foreign military attachés yesterday informed me
                    that long before July 7 the General Staff knew that the Japanese felt that their
                    military force at Fengtai was seriously exposed to attack and wished to get
                    possession of Nanyuan airdrome-Marco Polo Bridge areas. He said also that the
                    Chinese military headquarters in Peiping had reported to Nanking 10 days before
                    the bridge incident, and the Japanese authorities had warned Japanese in Peiping
                    that an event necessitating precautions on their part would occur before
                    long.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Peiping.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Peck</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
